WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.889
Solar systems follow a pretty generic recipe.

00:00:03.889 --> 00:00:07.540
To begin, combine hydrogen, helium, oxygen,

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.620
and carbon into a cloud of gas and dust.

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:14.200
Sprinkle with magnesium, iron and silicon.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.600
Spin until the cloud forms a star and a planetary disk.

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:20.260
Wait 10 million years,

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.560
or until lumps form in the disk.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:23.460
And voila!

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:23.960
[snap]

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.740
You’ve baked a solar system.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.480
But the planets we’ve found

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:28.640
orbiting other stars

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:30.260
are very different from the ones

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:31.580
in our own solar system.

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.200
So, the recipes for cooking up an exoplanet

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.580
are probably pretty different

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.620
from those for baking an Earth.

00:00:41.660 --> 00:00:43.700
Okay, so you’ve got a star.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:47.140
It contains a list of all the chemical ingredients

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.700
that the planet has to work with.

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.500
Those proportions vary from planet to planet,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:54.600
and astronomers are still trying to figure out

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.020
what factors produce a rocky planet

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.640
versus a gassy one.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:01.860
Mixing matters, too.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.920
Some planetary disks are more turbulent than others.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.380
And where different elements end up in the mix

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.060
determines whether or not they end up in a planet.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.140
When the planet forms also makes a big difference.

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:16.900
Gas giants need a lot of prep time.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.660
They have to form a rocky core

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.880
before they can start collecting vapors,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.860
so they start baking soon after their star forms.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:25.080
[ringing kitchen timer]

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.640
Small, rocky planets can start later.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.360
How far a planet forms in relation to its star

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.840
complicates planetary recipes.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.460
Small planets that form too close to a star

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.080
can lose their atmospheres

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:41.600
to energetic winds from the star.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:46.760
Sometimes big planets may arise at the system’s edges

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.900
but finish cooking closer to their star later on.

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.060
That’s one way to make oddballs

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:54.920
like hot versions of Jupiter or Neptune.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.100
Scientists are studying exactly how these variables

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.340
influence planet formation in other systems.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.540
And NASA’s latest planet-hunting satellite TESS

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.720
could reveal clues to exotic exoplanet compositions.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:13.960
Meanwhile, researchers are cooking up

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.960
experimental planets in the lab,

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:18.860
to try to understand how other systems formed.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:20.860
[music]

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:21.820
[snap]

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.100
[snap]

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:26.280
So one day, baking an exoplanet

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:28.300
will be a piece of cake.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.300
[ music]

